// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One GUI: label and one TOOLTIP: line per row of TheOptionCatalog.  A label
// and the combo box under it share the tooltip; an OPTION_ENUM row also needs
// its entries, numbered from zero in the order the enum declares them.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

GUI:WindowMode
"Window Mode"
END

GUI:WindowMode0
"Fullscreen"
END

GUI:WindowMode1
"Borderless"
END

GUI:WindowMode2
"Windowed"
END

TOOLTIP:WindowMode
"Fullscreen covers the monitor at the resolution above, scaled up to it when smaller, and steps aside on alt-tab. Borderless always draws at the monitor's own resolution and stays put behind other windows."
END

GUI:ResolutionNote0
"Monitor supports this mode"
END

GUI:FullscreenScaling
"Fullscreen Fit"
END

GUI:FullscreenScaling0
"Stretch to fill"
END

GUI:FullscreenScaling1
"Keep shape, add bars"
END

TOOLTIP:FullscreenScaling
"How fullscreen fills a monitor whose shape your resolution does not have, such as 1920x1080 on a 1920x1200 screen or 1024x768 on a widescreen. Stretch fills every pixel; the other keeps the picture's proportions with black bars."
END

GUI:Monitor
"Monitor"
END

TOOLTIP:Monitor
"The monitor the game plays on. The resolution list below holds the sizes this monitor can show. Applied when you Accept."
END

GUI:MSAA
"Antialiasing"
END

GUI:MSAA0
"Off"
END

GUI:MSAA1
"2x"
END

GUI:MSAA2
"4x"
END

GUI:MSAA3
"8x"
END

GUI:MSAA4
"16x"
END

TOOLTIP:MSAA
"Smooths the stair steps along model edges. Only the Direct3D 9 renderer uses it: the default renderer smooths edges on its own and ignores this setting. A level your card does not offer drops to the highest one it does. Applies the next time the game starts."
END

GUI:Bloom
"Glow"
END

GUI:Bloom0
"Off"
END

GUI:Bloom1
"Low"
END

GUI:Bloom2
"Medium"
END

GUI:Bloom3
"High"
END

GUI:Bloom4
"Ultra"
END

TOOLTIP:Bloom
"How hard fire, explosions, muzzle flashes and lasers burn and how far their light spills over what is around them. Buildings, the sky, snow and sand never glow. Off is the look the artwork was painted for. The Direct3D 9 renderer gets a softer version, and under Classic Graphics only the laser halos follow it."
END

GUI:VSync
"Vertical Sync"
END

TOOLTIP:VSync
"Holds the picture to the monitor so it does not tear. Off, the picture runs as fast as the machine draws. Applied when you Accept."
END

GUI:ClassicGraphics
"Classic Graphics"
END

TOOLTIP:ClassicGraphics
"The game as it looked in 2003: its own textures and ground, the old hard shadows, no bump detail and no glow or sharpening. The game opens this way. Untick it for the Reforged picture: the High preset fills in the boxes, and you can move the slider from there. Takes effect the next time the game starts."
END

GUI:InfantryShadows
"Infantry Shadows"
END

TOOLTIP:InfantryShadows
"Soldiers and the other animated models cast real shadows too. Needs Cast Shadows. Applies from the next map."
END

GUI:ProjectileShadows
"Missile Shadows"
END

TOOLTIP:ProjectileShadows
"Missiles and bombs the artists gave no shadow get one on the ground. Applies from the next map."
END

GUI:PropShadows
"Scenery Shadows"
END

TOOLTIP:PropShadows
"Fences, rubbish and shrubs, which the original drew with no shadow at all, get one. Applies from the next map."
END

GUI:ParticleShadows
"Smoke Shadows"
END

TOOLTIP:ParticleShadows
"Big clouds of smoke and dust lay a soft shadow on the ground under them. Applies when you press Accept."
END

GUI:Smoke
"Smoke"
END

GUI:Smoke0
"Original"
END

GUI:Smoke1
"Thick"
END

GUI:Smoke2
"Very Thick"
END

TOOLTIP:Smoke
"How long smoke hangs in the air and how dense it looks. 175 of the game's 1088 effects are smoke. Very Thick cost 0.7 ms a frame over a burning column when it was measured. Takes effect the next time the game starts."
END

GUI:ParticleBounce
"Particles Hit The Ground"
END

TOOLTIP:ParticleBounce
"Sparks, debris and the other particles land and bounce on the terrain instead of falling through it. Takes effect the next time the game starts."
END

GUI:MiddleMousePans
"Middle Mouse Pans"
END

TOOLTIP:MiddleMousePans
"Hold the middle button to drag the map under the cursor."
END

GUI:ZoomToCursor
"Zoom To Cursor"
END

TOOLTIP:ZoomToCursor
"The wheel zooms towards whatever the cursor is over instead of the middle of the screen."
END

GUI:StartAtMaxZoom
"Start Zoomed Out"
END

TOOLTIP:StartAtMaxZoom
"A match opens with the camera as far out as the wheel goes. Off, it opens close in, at the height the original game used."
END

GUI:ScrollSpeed
"Camera Scroll Speed"
END

TOOLTIP:ScrollSpeed
"How fast the camera pans: at the screen edge, on the arrow keys and while you drag with the right mouse button. The mouse wheel has its own Zoom Speed. Takes effect on Accept."
END

GUI:ZoomSpeed
"Zoom Speed"
END

TOOLTIP:ZoomSpeed
"How far one turn of the mouse wheel moves the camera in or out. At 100 it moves as far as in the original game. Takes effect on Accept."
END

GUI:DragTolerance
"Drag Threshold"
END

TOOLTIP:DragTolerance
"How many pixels the mouse may move with a button held before a click becomes a drag. Raise it if selection boxes start by accident, lower it if drags read as clicks."
END

GUI:AmbientVolume
"Ambient Volume"
END

TOOLTIP:AmbientVolume
"Birds, wind, water and the hum of a town. Sound FX no longer moves these, so the battle can stay loud while the world around it stays quiet."
END

GUI:MasterVolume
"Master Volume"
END

TOOLTIP:MasterVolume
"Turns the whole game up or down: music, effects, voices, ambience and movies. The other sliders keep the mix, this one sets how loud the mix is."
END

GUI:OptionsGroupMusic
"Music"
END

GUI:DynamicMusic
"Music Follows the Fight"
END

TOOLTIP:DynamicMusic
"In a skirmish or online match the music answers the battle: one of your side's calmer tracks while nothing happens, a harder one once guns fire near the camera or at your forces, and back to calm a few seconds after the fight ends. Off leaves the music as the original game played it."
END

GUI:OptionsGroupOutput
"Output"
END

GUI:AudioDevice
"Output Device"
END

TOOLTIP:AudioDevice
"The speakers or headphones the game plays through. Default follows Windows and moves with it when you plug in a headset. Applied when you Accept."
END

GUI:AudioDeviceDefault
"Default"
END

GUI:SpeakerType
"Speaker Setup"
END

TOOLTIP:SpeakerType
"How the battlefield's sounds are placed around you. Headphones give each ear its own timing and tone, so a sound behind or beside you is heard there. 5.1 and 7.1 spread them over surround speakers; on Linux and macOS they play as stereo. Applied when you Accept."
END

GUI:SpeakerType0
"Stereo Speakers"
END

GUI:SpeakerType1
"Headphones"
END

GUI:SpeakerType2
"5.1 Surround"
END

GUI:SpeakerType3
"7.1 Surround"
END

GUI:MuteInBackground
"Mute in Background"
END

TOOLTIP:MuteInBackground
"Silences the game while another window has the focus. Off keeps it playing behind a browser or on a second monitor."
END

GUI:LegacySound
"Legacy Sound"
END

TOOLTIP:LegacySound
"The original game's sound: no pitch bend on passing sounds, no Aurora sonic boom, no cap on battle sounds or unit replies, no limiter, no map ambience and no music following the fight. Master volume, the device and the speakers still apply. Takes effect at once."
END

GUI:CameraShake
"Camera Shake"
END

TOOLTIP:CameraShake
"Blasts shake the camera. A nuke or a big building coming down nearby kicks it hard for a moment. Untick to keep the view still. Applies when you press Accept."
END

GUI:IsometricCamera
"Isometric Camera"
END

TOOLTIP:IsometricCamera
"The battlefield seen from far off, so a unit is the same size at the top of the screen as at the bottom. You still turn and zoom the camera."
END

GUI:EdgeScrollWindowed
"Edge Scroll In A Window"
END

TOOLTIP:EdgeScrollWindowed
"Retail refuses to scroll from the screen edge in windowed mode. Turn this on to get it back on a second monitor or in borderless."
END

GUI:SnapCamera45
"Snap Camera Rotation"
END

TOOLTIP:SnapCamera45
"Rotating the camera clicks to the eight 45 degree headings instead of stopping anywhere. Hold Shift while turning to get the other behaviour for that turn."
END

GUI:GridBuild
"Grid Building Placement"
END

TOOLTIP:GridBuild
"Structures land on the pathfinder's own 10-unit grid, drawn under the building while you place it, so two buildings put down by eye share an edge instead of leaving a gap too narrow to walk through. Off, only the cells nothing can be built on are marked."
END

GUI:NudgeBuild
"Nudge Blocked Placement"
END

TOOLTIP:NudgeBuild
"A structure whose spot is blocked slides to the nearest one it fits instead of going red under the cursor."
END

GUI:SnapBuild45
"Snap Building Rotation"
END

TOOLTIP:SnapBuild45
"Drag-to-rotate clicks to the eight 45 degree headings, which is what walls and defenses along a base edge want."
END

GUI:SnapBuildNeighbour
"Snap to Nearby Buildings"
END

TOOLTIP:SnapBuildNeighbour
"A structure put down a cell or so short of another one is pulled flush against its edge, so the two leave no gap."
END

GUI:RangeRing
"Placement Range Ring"
END

TOOLTIP:RangeRing
"Draws the weapon range of the structure you are about to place."
END

GUI:WorkersReturn
"Workers Return To Supply"
END

TOOLTIP:WorkersReturn
"A worker that finishes a building goes back to gathering instead of standing where it built."
END

GUI:BuildTooltips
"Detailed Build Tooltips"
END

TOOLTIP:BuildTooltips
"Build buttons list cost, build time and prerequisites."
END

GUI:HudOverlay
"HUD Overlay"
END

TOOLTIP:HudOverlay
"The extra readouts across the top of the screen: power, income and the production strip."
END

GUI:NetBox
"Info Box"
END

TOOLTIP:NetBox
"The box in the top right corner with the match clock, the frame rates and the connection. Off, the superweapon timers move up into the corner."
END

GUI:OptionsGroupHud
"HUD"
END

GUI:EmptyBuildingPips
"Empty Building Slots"
END

TOOLTIP:EmptyBuildingPips
"The row of empty slots over a building with nobody inside, like the ten over a Barracks. Off, a building shows its slots only while someone is in it."
END

GUI:ArchiveReplays
"Archive Replays"
END

TOOLTIP:ArchiveReplays
"Every game is kept under its own name instead of overwriting the last one."
END

GUI:HealthBars
"Health Bars"
END

GUI:HealthBars0
"Always"
END

GUI:HealthBars1
"Smart"
END

GUI:HealthBars2
"Selection Only"
END

GUI:HealthBars3
"Never"
END

TOOLTIP:HealthBars
"Smart shows a bar over anything hurt, plus the selection and whatever is under the cursor. Selection only is what retail did."
END

GUI:OptionsGroupInput
"Input"
END

GUI:ChromaLighting
"Razer Chroma Lighting"
END

TOOLTIP:ChromaLighting
"During a match, a Razer keyboard, mouse and mousepad show the command bar, power, superweapons and money. In the menus Synapse keeps its own lighting."
END

GUI:OrderLines
"Order Lines"
END

TOOLTIP:OrderLines
"A line from each selected unit to where it is going, and on through every point it has queued. Off, the markers on those points stay."
END

GUI:PlayerColors
"Player Colors"
END

GUI:PlayerColors0
"Original"
END

GUI:PlayerColors1
"You, Allies, Enemies"
END

GUI:PlayerColors2
"By Team"
END

TOOLTIP:PlayerColors
"You, allies, enemies paints you blue, everyone on your side green and everyone shooting at you red. By team gives each alliance its own color and each member of it a shade. Your own screen only, so it changes nothing for anyone else in the game."
END

// Language - the Gameplay page's second group.  Each entry is written in its own language, so a
